WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.517 --> 00:00:04.850
WESTON HUTCHINS: Welcome to
"Cloud OnAir," live webinars

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.080
from Google Cloud.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.119
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:09.590
My name is Weston Hutchins.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:11.570
I'm a product manager on GKE.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:13.290
INES ENVID: And my
name is Ines Envid.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.045
I'm a product manager in GKE as
well, focusing on networking.

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:17.420
WESTON HUTCHINS:
And today, we're

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.190
talking about three
reasons why you

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.650
should run your enterprise
workloads on Google Kubernetes

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:22.710
Engine.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.120
So you can ask questions at
any time on the platform.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.100
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.520
With that, let's get started.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.400
So it's no secret that
containers are generating

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.290
more and more buzz every day.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.810
And it's pretty much a
requirement for enterprises

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:42.380
today to have containers as
part of their long-term IT

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:44.150
roadmap and strategy.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.640
[? Gardner ?]
estimates that by 2020,

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.460
50% of global organizations
will be running

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.560
containerized
workloads in production

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.500
across a wide variety of
industries, including finance,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.050
media, manufacturing, and more.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.810
So as part of any strong
container management platform,

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.010
you need to make
sure that Kubernetes

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.150
is in the conversation for
orchestrating those workloads

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.280
as you start to move
from virtual machines

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.734
over to a container-based
infrastructure.

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:14.900
Now, for those that aren't
familiar with Kubernetes,

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.459
this isn't going to be a deep
dive or anything like that.

00:01:18.459 --> 00:01:20.750
But I did want to at least
talk a little bit about some

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.150
of the benefits that Kubernetes
gives you out of the box.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:28.610
Kubernetes is an open, portable,
container-centric management

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:29.720
platform.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.780
And a lot of what
Kubernetes does

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.800
is build a platform to
help you extend and manage

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.680
your applications
that are containerized

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.110
on the underlying
infrastructure that you have.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.167
For me, Kubernetes is really
two main key components.

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.500
The first one is that it's
an infrastructure abstraction

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.310
layer.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.980
When you go and create
a Kubernetes cluster,

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.920
Kubernetes hides a lot of the
underlying infrastructure,

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.860
things like networking,
storage, compute.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.580
You don't really have to think
about the nuances of that

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.340
from platform to platform.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.960
Kubernetes gives you a
standardized infrastructure

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.750
layer to program your
applications against.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.300
The second piece
is that it's a set

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.210
of declarative APIs for managing
application-centric workloads.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.959
So as you start to
roll out applications,

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:18.500
there are a number
of things that you

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.560
need to handle, including
things like making

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.050
your application available,
health checking, making sure

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.990
that you're load balancing
between different services

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.220
in your cluster.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.965
And one of the nice
things about Kubernetes

00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:32.780
is that it has
built-in primitives

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.660
to handle a lot of those
application management

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:39.620
features for you automatically.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.380
Kubernetes can be a little
complex, especially when

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.340
you're getting started.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.965
But if you think
about what it normally

00:02:44.965 --> 00:02:48.140
takes to run a production
grade application,

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.570
there is a ton of things
that Kubernetes gives you out

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.430
of the box just to make
your life a lot simpler.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.900
So just to cherry pick a few of
these, looking at scheduling.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.590
So across the fleet of
instances that I have set up

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.540
around the world,
how do I decide

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.670
where to take
containers and pods

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:07.250
and schedule those across
the different nodes?

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.380
How do I ensure that my
applications are always

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.540
up-to-date and
healthy, even though I

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.240
know that failures can occur?

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.920
Kubernetes allows you to
go and restart those pods

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.720
and restart those
applications when

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.820
things don't go quite right.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.340
We handle things like load
balancing and scaling.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.170
Kubernetes has
built-in primitives

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:27.440
for making sure that
your cluster is always

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.590
able to handle the load
that hits it on peak hours,

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:33.230
and then scale back down
during off-peak hours

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.860
to save you money when you're
running your cluster at scale.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.930
It has built-in primitives
for storage, volumes

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:41.360
that work across a number of
different cloud providers,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.990
and gives you a nice, portable
abstraction layer to say,

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:47.170
I want disk storage
and have that work

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:51.406
well across GCP and AWS and
even on on-prem systems.

00:03:51.406 --> 00:03:53.780
And then it includes a lot of
built-in tools and services

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.970
on top of everything that
Kubernetes gives you out

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.030
of the box, so things like
logging and monitoring.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.460
There's a ton of extensions
and pluggable frameworks

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.740
for making your
cluster observable

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.650
and making your apps observable
within those clusters.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.040
So while Kubernetes can
feel a bit daunting,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.350
I think as people
start to get ramped up

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:13.910
with the platform
around Kubernetes,

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:17.160
you'll see that a lot of these
features that Kubernetes gives

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.910
you means that you don't have
to think about these yourself

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:21.800
when you're rolling
out your containers.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.960
So what we've actually
seen is an increase

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.270
in usage in Kubernetes
quite substantially.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.805
The rapid adoption
has been dramatic.

00:04:31.805 --> 00:04:33.927
I went to KubeCon
here in Seattle

00:04:33.927 --> 00:04:35.510
just a couple of
years ago and there's

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.750
been a 10x increase from the
latest KubeCon that just was

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:44.240
hosted in Copenhagen. And recent
surveys suggest that over 54%

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.800
of Fortune 100 companies
are using Kubernetes

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.260
in production today.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.246
So there's really no
better time to get started

00:04:50.246 --> 00:04:51.620
experimenting with
the containers

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.630
and using Kubernetes
as your orchestration

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:56.240
platform of choice.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.270
But by far, the easiest way
to get started with Kubernetes

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:02.890
is to use Google's fully
managed Kubernetes solution,

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:04.720
Kubernetes Engine.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.720
Some of the biggest benefits
to using Kubernetes Engine

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.510
is that you can start your
cluster with a single click.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.640
And once you have that
cluster provisioned,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:15.250
you can view all of the
workloads in the Google Cloud

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.590
console from this
beautiful UI screen.

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.470
So you can see things
like what applications

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.170
are running across my cluster,
what services are there,

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:25.780
how do I set up service
discovery and load

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.690
balancing across the different
services within the cluster,

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:32.410
and even start to tweak things
like configuration of secrets

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.510
and config maps that
are in your clusters

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:37.660
all from the Google
Cloud Platform console.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.700
But by far the
best part about GKE

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.520
and the one that we hear
repeatedly from customers

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.790
is that Google manages the
cluster for you so you don't

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.720
have to manage it yourself.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:49.660
There are a lot of
day two operations

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.490
that are just
challenging, things

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.220
like making sure
that etcd is scaled,

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.230
making sure that
etcd is backed up,

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.970
having high
availability components

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:01.390
as part of your control plan
that can resist outages.

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.770
There's a lot of
domain knowledge

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.770
that comes in managing
a Kubernetes cluster

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.260
and Google Kubernetes
Engine handles a lot

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:11.290
of that hard work for you.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.600
So in this webinar,
we're going to primarily

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.090
be focusing on a few of the
attributes and considerations

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:18.520
that you need to think
about when you're starting

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:22.270
to move from a VM world over to
something built on containers

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:23.740
and Kubernetes.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:25.690
There are a lot of
fundamental questions

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:27.920
that you need to start
thinking through, such as,

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.890
how does Kubernetes fit
into the operational model

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:31.930
that I already have?

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.870
I have people that are used
to doing approval flows

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.140
and provisioning machines.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:40.494
Can Kubernetes fit into
that system very well?

00:06:40.494 --> 00:06:42.160
There's a bunch of
other things that you

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.118
need to start thinking
about, like scalability,

00:06:44.118 --> 00:06:46.850
availability, security.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.060
And for this webinar
in particular,

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.550
we're just going to be
focusing on three reasons

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.800
why you should start
thinking about Kubernetes

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.320
and especially start thinking
about using Google Kubernetes

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:57.256
Engine.

00:06:57.256 --> 00:06:59.380
So the three key areas
we're going to be looking at

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:02.470
are reliability,
availability, and how

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:05.290
Kubernetes and GKE fits
into your company's

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:08.240
operational model.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.310
So on reliability,
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:12.987
is already trusted by
many enterprises today.

00:07:12.987 --> 00:07:15.070
And the reason is that
we've been doing containers

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:17.860
at scale for more than
a decade right now.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.590
Internally, containers
have been used

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.650
as Borg, our internal
container orchestration

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.650
system all the way since 2006.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.110
And then we started to
see containers really

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:31.870
pick up mind share in
the developer community.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.720
So Docker was announced
in 2013 and started

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:35.980
to get a lot of traction.

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:37.480
And this is when
we started thinking

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:39.520
about how we could
help accelerate

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.150
the container-based application
environment by offering

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.370
primitives that helped
you run applications

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:46.810
built on containers.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.970
And this is really where
we took the lessons

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.280
we've learned from our
internal system board

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:54.150
and started applying
it towards Kubernetes.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:59.980
And around 2015 is when the
1.0 GA of Kubernetes launched.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:01.750
And shortly after
that, we launched

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.780
the general availability of
our fully managed offering,

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.300
Kubernetes Engine.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:11.300
And so the key message
here is that we've not only

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.270
been doing containers
for a long time,

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:15.520
we've been doing
Kubernetes for a long time.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.010
So ever since
Kubernetes hit GA, we've

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:21.370
had a fully managed solution on
top of Google's Cloud Platform.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.800
And we've learned a ton
by managing and operating

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:25.631
this over that period.

00:08:25.631 --> 00:08:27.130
So there's a ton
of domain knowledge

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:30.430
that our SRE team has and our
dev has about what it takes

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:31.840
to run Kubernetes at scale.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.030
Some of the nice features
that GKE gives you out

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:38.650
of the box, in terms
of reliability,

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:41.169
is just making sure
that your cluster is--

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:43.750
you can trust Google to
run your cluster for you.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:45.190
So we do a lot of
things where we

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:48.610
take the control plane and the
complexities of running that,

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:50.770
and hand that over to
our SRE team and a lot

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:56.272
of the automation we've built
to run Kubernetes here on GCP.

00:08:56.272 --> 00:08:57.730
So some of the
features we give you

00:08:57.730 --> 00:08:59.380
are a managed
control plane, where

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.290
you can turn on features like
auto-repair and auto-upgrades,

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.510
which I'll talk
about in a minute.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.150
We do a ton of testing
on Kubernetes versions

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:09.970
and node operating systems
to ensure compatibility.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:12.160
And we have a long list
of configuration matrices

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:13.750
that we're always
testing to make sure

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:16.270
that we're rolling out new
versions of Kubernetes that

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:19.330
will not break your
existing cluster.

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:22.120
One of the really key features
of using something like GKE

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:25.150
is the deep integration that
we have with the [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:26.780
Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.690
So we have an amazing
[INAUDIBLE] system

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:31.090
that allows you to boot
our cluster faster than any

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:32.660
of the other cloud providers.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:37.060
We have deep integration with
our networking and VPC stack,

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:39.140
which Ines will be
talking about shortly.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.980
And then you can get features
like CI/CD, Container Builder

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:45.560
registry, logging and
monitoring tool in Stackdriver,

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:47.440
to just give you that
overall confidence

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.440
in operational ability
to consistently deploy

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.770
applications to your cluster and
then monitor those production

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:58.120
apps as they're out there
in your GKE cluster.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:09:59.920
But by far, and I
mentioned this before,

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.450
the most important
thing is that we

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:04.840
have a set of SREs that are
always monitoring your cluster

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:06.220
and making changes.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:08.200
Some of the actions
that they perform

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:12.460
are keeping etcd backed up and
recovered in case of a disaster

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.040
recovery, monitoring
for global outages

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.545
and making sure that we can
respond to those quickly,

00:10:17.545 --> 00:10:19.420
and then alerting you
when there are problems

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.220
in the cluster that
you may be hitting,

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.050
like scalability
limits of Kubernetes,

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.330
such as too many name
spaces or too many services

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:26.590
that you've created.

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.240
And all this comes
out of the box

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.270
just by using GKE's platform.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:34.090
So now, I want to talk
about availability.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:37.780
And we recently launched
a number of new features

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:39.400
around availability.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.310
These features are regional
clusters, node auto-repair,

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:46.840
and regional persistent disks,
which just went to beta.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.500
The first feature that I want
to cover is regional clusters.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:53.710
So we announced the
GA of this last week.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.410
Regional clusters
is an improvement

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.740
on these zonal clusters that
we were offering for GKE.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:02.380
And what it does is it
spreads your control plane

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:04.530
across three zones in a region.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:07.270
And it also spreads your
nodes across three zones

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:11.090
in a region to give your app
increased availability as well.

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:12.580
All of this
communication happens

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:16.700
through a highly tolerant
load balancer system

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:18.580
so that even if one of
these were to go down,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.580
you could always shift
traffic over to a new zone.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.000
So the biggest benefits with
having this high availability

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.700
multi-master setup is
that you get an increased

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:32.680
SLO, an increased up
time as part of GKE,

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.840
where you get three
and a half [? nines. ?]

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.330
But by far, the feature that
we hear most of the customers

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:40.270
really wanted was this ability
to do zero downtime control

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:41.950
plane upgrades.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:46.510
So as we start rolling out
new versions of Kubernetes,

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:50.020
we will actually do a one by
one upgrade of your zonal--

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.270
or sorry-- of your
regional control plane

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:55.050
and upgrade each zone at a
time so that your control plane

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.160
is always having the
ability to serve traffic

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.450
for your applications.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:03.550
As a quick aside, I do
want to talk a little bit

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.290
about upgrade strategies.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:07.690
And these are just
general best practice tips

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:11.200
when you're thinking about,
how do I keep my app updated

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:13.245
and highly available
during an upgrade?

00:12:13.245 --> 00:12:15.370
So I think one of the first
tips is using something

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.880
like regional clusters to make
sure you have zero downtime,

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:22.120
but let's cover a few other
general best practices here.

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:25.070
So just a brief primer
on the architecture,

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.310
the way Google Kubernetes
Engine is set up

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:29.200
is that you have a
control plane, which

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:31.852
is a set of master nodes
that are managed by Google.

00:12:31.852 --> 00:12:33.310
And then you have
your nodes, which

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.770
are controlled by you
in your own project that

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:37.840
run your workloads.

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:39.820
And from the control
plane aspect,

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:42.730
Google manages the
upgrades for you.

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:45.070
So we will automatically
upgrade the control plane

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:47.774
as new versions of
Kubernetes are available.

00:12:47.774 --> 00:12:49.690
Now, the thing to note
is that we don't always

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:53.080
upgrade every cluster to the
latest version of Kubernetes.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.450
We typically pick a stable
version that's usually

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.170
one or two releases behind.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:59.500
However, you as
the customer always

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:02.350
have the option of trying the
latest and greatest by clicking

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:05.170
the upgrade available link
inside the Google Cloud console

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:07.325
or running it from
a G Cloud command.

00:13:07.325 --> 00:13:08.950
And the most important
piece about this

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:10.776
is you have control
over when you

00:13:10.776 --> 00:13:12.400
want to upgrade to
the newest versions,

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:14.710
but we will always
make sure that security

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:16.750
patches and other
fixes are performed

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:18.890
on your cluster for
you automatically.

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.680
And as I mentioned
before, if you really

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:22.180
want to have increased
availability,

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:25.950
use zero downtime
during upgrade,

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:28.330
as it's a much better
model for maintaining

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:31.270
the up time of your cluster.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.070
So past the control
plane, you need

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:35.570
to think about how you're going
to keep your nodes upgraded,

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:38.200
to make sure specifically that
they're available while you're

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.550
serving traffic.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:41.980
So there's a couple features
that I wanted to mention.

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.350
First off, you can choose
to turn on auto-upgrade

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:45.230
for your cluster.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:46.938
And this is particularly
what I recommend

00:13:46.938 --> 00:13:50.410
for customers that want to
make sure that their cluster is

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:53.140
always kept up-to-date with
the latest security patches.

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:55.150
Or if you want more
control, you can

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:57.370
choose to turn off that
feature and manually upgrade

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:00.580
your cluster as newer
versions are supported.

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:03.490
One feature that you can use
to control when upgrades happen

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:04.810
is called maintenance windows.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:07.351
And this feature allows you to
set a schedule and a four-hour

00:14:07.351 --> 00:14:09.790
window of which you
say upgrades are

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:12.020
allowed to happen during
this particular time period.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:14.020
And that just gives you
a little more confidence

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.820
that your cluster will always
be able to serve peak traffic,

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.810
but then we can keep things
upgraded when less load is

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:21.520
hitting the cluster.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:23.410
But in terms of
upgrade strategy,

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.600
there's two common
patterns that I've seen

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.460
about keeping nodes up-to-date.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.230
The first is just
what GKE gives you out

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:31.390
of the box, which
is a rolling update.

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:35.600
If you go and click
the button showing

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:37.100
in the bottom
right-hand corner here

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.669
around node upgrades
are available,

00:14:38.669 --> 00:14:40.210
it'll walk you
through a wizard where

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:44.020
we will upgrade your cluster
one node at a time in a rolling

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:45.190
upgrade fashion.

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:47.470
This is nice just because
it's simple and easy,

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:49.630
but it does have one
big downside in that you

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.640
need to make sure
that your cluster can

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:55.340
operate with one reduced node
during that upgrade process.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:15:00.280
So we will constantly be taking
a node down, draining it,

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:03.220
and then adding it back with
a newer version of Kubernetes.

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:05.050
One way to handle this
is just to provision

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:07.540
in an extra capacity during
your upgrade process.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:09.670
And we hope to make this
more automatic and easier

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:12.370
in the future.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:15.010
The second common
strategy that I see

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:16.910
is migration with node pools.

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:19.180
So one of the nice
things about node pools

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:22.090
is that it allows you to
create a group of nodes

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:25.150
on a newer version of Kubernetes
and do a form of traffic

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:27.400
shifting over to
that new version.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.610
So what you can do is create
a second node pool as part

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:34.810
of the same cluster on a newer
version and then one by one,

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.926
use [? kubectl ?] to
go and drain the nodes,

00:15:36.926 --> 00:15:38.300
and then [? cord ?]
on the nodes,

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.780
which will then start to
reschedule pods on the newer

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.400
version of Kubernetes.

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.710
And this allows you a
little bit more control

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:46.299
over the upgrade
process and also

00:15:46.299 --> 00:15:48.340
gives you the ability to
roll back in case things

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:49.600
start going wrong.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.940
So this is a really
nice process that you

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:54.340
can use in order to go
and upgrade your clusters

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.590
to the newest versions.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:59.260
One other feature that
we just announced GA for

00:15:59.260 --> 00:16:00.710
is auto-repair.

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.130
Now, your Kubernetes
cluster, your nodes

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:05.260
can go down for a
number of reasons.

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:07.000
There can be kernel bugs.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.530
They can be unreachable.

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:11.620
You can have out of memory
or out of storage exceptions.

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:14.010
And what auto-repair
does is it constantly

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:16.330
is monitoring and health
checking your nodes.

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:19.630
And if it detects a problem
such as a node not responding,

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.610
it will go and
respin up those nodes

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.620
and keep that
cluster up-to-date.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.120
What we found from
customers is this

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:26.816
is usually a feature
that they love

00:16:26.816 --> 00:16:28.190
to just have on
in the background

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:29.731
and constantly
keeping those clusters

00:16:29.731 --> 00:16:33.950
up as problems are introduced.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:35.890
The other new feature
that we just announced

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:39.790
is regional persistent disk
support for Kubernetes Engine.

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:43.600
Now, one of the nice things
about regional persistent disks

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:47.770
is that typically, doing
distributed storage replication

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:49.130
is really challenging.

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:51.520
And what regional persistent
volumes give you the ability

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:55.840
to do is automatically replicate
between two zones in a region.

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:58.570
So rather than having
to think about how

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:01.810
do I shard my application
such that it's automatically

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:04.000
replicating across
different zones,

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.069
you can just replicate
at the storage layer

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:09.230
and have Google handle all of
that for you automatically.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:11.829
So this is a huge time
saver and complexity reducer

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:13.569
for those that want
to have the increased

00:17:13.569 --> 00:17:15.819
availability with having
storage replicated

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:18.730
across multiple
zones in a region.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.980
And we've really seen
a lot of customers

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:24.220
come to depend and rely on the
high up time and availability

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:27.400
of GKE, especially around
events like Black Friday,

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:29.860
where we can see a massive
increase in traffic.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.110
We have gone through
this many, many years

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:34.180
and are able to scale
up our clusters in order

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:37.500
to meet customer demand.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.680
And with that, I will
hand it over to Ines.

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:41.890
INES ENVID: Thanks
so much, Weston.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:45.390
I want this, well, then to
focus on the operational model

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:48.090
for your Kubernetes deployment.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.440
And the reason why
I'll be focusing

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.410
on the networking, the
reason why this is important

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:58.360
is because enterprises run
their Kubernetes deployment

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.650
as part of the rest
of their deployments.

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:03.970
And we need to make
sure that everything

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:09.910
that we build, in terms of
the models fit into the VPC

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:12.340
and to a scale the
current deployments,

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:14.690
they also work for the
Kubernetes deployments.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:17.680
So I'm going to be focusing
on a couple of very

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:22.310
important foundational
concepts that we have released

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:24.970
and that are going to allow
us to really provide you

00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:26.830
with the tools and
the models that you'll

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:30.470
need for your deployment.

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.200
So I want to back
up a little bit

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:35.410
and talk about
Google VPC network.

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:37.950
There is a way this
is significant,

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:42.040
is because it really
provides very significant

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:46.660
advantages over some
other networking models.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:52.600
So we have been
already work as Google,

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:56.800
we have significant expertise
on building global networks.

00:18:56.800 --> 00:19:01.450
And we have done so for
our own business areas.

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:04.600
As we built our
public cloud model,

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:08.170
we really leveraged from
that global networking model,

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:13.150
exposing through the VPC the
possibility for you as a user

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:16.720
to build your global
piece of Google network

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:19.120
for your enterprises.

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:20.710
The reason why
that is significant

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:24.070
is because it
significantly reduced

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:26.830
the amount of operational
burden that you

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:29.100
will have on having
to [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.690
regional connectivity
domains and having that

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:36.250
offered to you by default.
It will as well mitigate

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:38.800
the fact that you need
to connect separately

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:42.850
from your data center, say,
if you have on-prem systems,

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:46.720
through separate connections,
like Google Cloud network will

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:49.330
offer you a single entry
point for all your deployments

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:50.410
globally.

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:52.690
And as well, it allows
you a centralized model

00:19:52.690 --> 00:19:57.730
for having your security
and policy construct.

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:03.940
Now, when we're applying that
to Kubernetes, we are really

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:05.680
making sure that
everything that we

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:09.670
have built for any
type of deployments,

00:20:09.670 --> 00:20:16.370
it works natively on Kubernetes.

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:19.370
So with that, I'm
going to introduce

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:25.180
the first key construct that you
will need for your Kubernetes

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:27.000
deployment.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.440
It is what we call native
VPC Kubernetes Engine

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:33.230
clusters with Alias IP.

00:20:33.230 --> 00:20:34.970
The reason why
this is significant

00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:39.710
is because when we are working
with container deployments,

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:43.350
really the granularity at which
we are providing networking

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:47.384
functions is not any more the
granularity of a VM instance.

00:20:47.384 --> 00:20:48.800
You need to provide
the networking

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:52.600
functions and the granularity
of IPs with represent spots.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:55.160
And that, as a conjunction,
represents services

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:57.810
that get exposed to a user.

00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:01.010
Now, when we are monitoring
the networking functions

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:06.560
for those spots and services,
things like IP allocation,

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:12.160
they are done through their
native VPC GKE clusters.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:17.090
They're done natively as
part of our GCP model.

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:22.310
You can allocate, as part of
your VPC subnet, primary range

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:27.080
that it serves as the IP
allocation range from which

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:29.930
you are allocating IP for pods.

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:31.790
Then you can have
a secondary range

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:34.280
that you use for allocating
your service's IPs.

00:21:34.280 --> 00:21:36.620
You can keep those
two separated.

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:39.140
But the nice thing is that by
doing the allocation as part

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:43.020
of your VPC model, you don't
need to have two separate IP

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:45.890
allocation systems,
one for your pods

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:48.170
and your service as part
of your GKE deployments,

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:51.830
another for your VMs as the
underlying infrastructure.

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:56.630
Additionally, the ability
to program directly these

00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:59.020
pods as part of our
VPC infrastructure,

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:01.670
it makes sure that we
are addressing natively

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:05.930
those spots and those services,
program in our networking

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:06.680
stack.

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.050
That allows us to scale better.

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:12.820
It provides us with
advantages like security

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:16.010
because we're able to understand
which spots are allocated

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:17.540
in which VM instances.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:21.440
And therefore, we're allowed to
implicitly apply anti-spoofing,

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:24.830
making sure that the
IPs from the pods

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:27.710
are really the
ones that are being

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:29.850
configured by you as a user.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:33.350
And in general, it really
gives us the foundational block

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:36.260
to provide every single
networking service,

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:39.840
whether we're talking about
load balancing functions,

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:43.400
whether we're talking
about routing, exposing

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:45.450
those routes to the internet.

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:50.030
Everything, as part of the GKE
clusters, pods, and services,

00:22:50.030 --> 00:22:54.380
are treated the same as we
will do with our VM instance

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:55.263
deployment.

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:03.320
Let's now talk about
the operational needs

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.030
for your Kubernetes Engine.

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:09.520
So we have seen the
following paradigm

00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:13.270
when deploying Kubernetes
Engine clusters.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:17.860
On one side, there is
usually network and security

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:20.470
administration that needs
to have some control,

00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:26.140
if only to understand what are
the rules of connectivity, what

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:30.250
are firewalls, and what are the
security policies through which

00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:31.892
the deployments
have been governed

00:23:31.892 --> 00:23:33.100
as part of those deployments.

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:35.980
So there is a need for
having a centralized view

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:40.630
and a centralized admin for
certain very sensitive security

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:43.150
policies and routing policies.

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:47.440
On the other hand, you have the
paradigm in which dev op teams,

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.680
they need to be agile enough
so that they have the autonomy

00:23:50.680 --> 00:23:55.720
to be able to deploy and to
operate their applications

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:58.630
without having to have
a very tight dependency

00:23:58.630 --> 00:24:02.800
on the infrastructure and
those administrative policies.

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:06.400
So we see requirements,
so for those dev ops

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:09.880
to have separate billing,
separate quarters, separate IM

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:13.420
control so they can
be able to operate

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:18.470
their apps without stepping
into each other's feet.

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:22.372
The last is that when you're
deploying your GKE apps,

00:24:22.372 --> 00:24:23.830
you want to make
sure that they are

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:25.460
able to communicate privately.

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:26.950
The reason why this
is important is

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:32.807
because when you're having a
microservice set of environment

00:24:32.807 --> 00:24:34.390
and you're breaking
your [INAUDIBLE],,

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:36.790
it's very important that
those apps are still

00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:38.380
able to communicate
without having

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:40.590
to be exposed to the internet.

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:43.030
And these requirements
are very important when

00:24:43.030 --> 00:24:45.580
you have CI/CD
environments in which you

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:49.270
want to promote certain
apps from deployment

00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:52.142
to test, to staging
and production.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:57.500
So with that, I want
to back up a little bit

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.830
because the way these
problems have been resolved

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:01.970
has been suboptimal.

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:06.620
And that is where Google
Cloud is stepping up

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:10.110
to provide a solution that
really makes those requirements

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:15.350
with a solution that is suitable
for your operational needs.

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:18.570
So why those deployments
have been suboptimal?

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:22.310
So for example, one
very traditional way

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:24.110
of deploying GKE
clusters, it will

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:30.440
be to have it in your own
account, in your own project

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:35.330
so that you can have your
control for billing, for quota,

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:40.280
for identity and access
management policies restricted

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:44.050
to that cluster so that you have
the separations, its cluster

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:46.280
and each set of developers.

00:25:46.280 --> 00:25:48.830
Then they're going
to be separated

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:50.990
by these type of controls.

00:25:50.990 --> 00:25:57.150
And then they communicate
through VPC control.

00:25:57.150 --> 00:26:02.250
But if you are really
deploying an application A

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:05.350
and application B as
part of the same cluster,

00:26:05.350 --> 00:26:09.560
you are really
losing the control

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:11.400
on some of those policies.

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:14.520
So you get the communication
through the VPC network,

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:16.460
like in this
picture, but then you

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:20.706
are losing the ability
for those developers

00:26:20.706 --> 00:26:22.830
to have their own quota,
to have their own billing.

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:28.720
The other option is now,
you separate them fully.

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:31.920
You have application A, which is
really restricted to a cluster,

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:33.190
to a single account.

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:34.840
Then you have
application B, which

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:37.300
is restricted to another
cluster, to another account.

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:41.567
But now, the VPCs that you
really connect those clusters

00:26:41.567 --> 00:26:43.150
to, which provide
you your [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:45.700
connectivity domain,
are separated.

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:48.520
You're not really able
to communicate easily,

00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:49.550
those applications.

00:26:49.550 --> 00:26:50.800
And then you create silos.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:53.320
So you are trading
off the communication

00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:58.060
that you had in the previous
picture with the able

00:26:58.060 --> 00:27:01.030
to control each of
the applications

00:27:01.030 --> 00:27:02.350
that you gain in this picture.

00:27:02.350 --> 00:27:05.980
In none of them you
have the optimal setup

00:27:05.980 --> 00:27:08.860
for being able to separate
policies and control

00:27:08.860 --> 00:27:11.350
for those clusters, but
having private connectivity

00:27:11.350 --> 00:27:12.430
without creating silos.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:19.630
That is where the model for
the shared VPC comes in place.

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:23.360
And if you see at the
deployment that we

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:27.780
have here, what it allows you
is to really have your clusters

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:31.040
deployed separately
in different projects

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:33.350
that will have separate
billing accounts

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:39.080
for those applications, separate
quarter, separate identity

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:44.300
and administration, and
identity management.

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:46.700
Those policies are
still completely

00:27:46.700 --> 00:27:50.180
separated for application
A and application B,

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:53.320
but there is still
a single VPC network

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:59.420
that is able to connect
both applications.

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:00.890
And not only is
it able to connect

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:03.380
privately both
applications, but it's also

00:28:03.380 --> 00:28:06.800
able to provide a common
space in which there

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:11.850
can be a set of route, a set
of firewalls that are globally

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:15.920
administered by a security
networking admin that can then

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:20.260
gain control on how these
applications are communicating.

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:22.850
Now, it provides
you a single place

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:25.880
in which you can understand
what are the communication rules

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:27.620
and what are the
security policies

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:31.850
for those applications, without
having these dev ops having

00:28:31.850 --> 00:28:34.460
to care about what
that connectivity

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:35.630
or what those rules are.

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:44.520
So to recap, when you are
deploying your applications

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:48.520
as part of your GKE deployment,
it is very important

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:51.220
that your developer should
be able to autonomously

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:56.370
develop and be able to
test and deploy and operate

00:28:56.370 --> 00:29:01.220
their applications without
stepping on each other's toes.

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:04.370
Now, the developers are really
good at doing their job.

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:07.960
They're really good at having
their applications develop,

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:13.700
testing them, making
sure that they perform.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:15.880
They should not be
responsible of having

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:18.520
to set up the networking,
the underlying networking

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:21.010
connectivity to
connect the others.

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:23.770
They should not have to
be responsible of how

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:28.210
do I now make my
connectivity available

00:29:28.210 --> 00:29:30.880
to this other application that
I need to communicate with.

00:29:30.880 --> 00:29:33.010
That is where the shared
VPC comes in place.

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:34.600
It gives us security
in networking.

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:36.820
I mean, not only
a place to control

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:40.210
that is very similar to the
traditional enterprise models,

00:29:40.210 --> 00:29:43.750
but it gives you a way to enable
your developers for really

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:46.030
setting up for them
those communication

00:29:46.030 --> 00:29:47.980
and those security
policy rules so

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:51.190
that it will make their
life really easy for them.

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:59.930
And just to recap, we have
seen tremendous traction

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:01.920
on adopting these
enterprise models.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:04.130
We've got a lot of
feedback from lots

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:06.710
of enterprises in which the
shared VPC model is really

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:10.550
the choice for their large
enterprise deployments.

00:30:10.550 --> 00:30:16.970
It's starting to be able to
achieve an operational model

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:19.280
that doesn't come up
with the trade-offs

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:21.575
that we have seen in the past.

00:30:21.575 --> 00:30:28.280
And as well, we have
seen the customers

00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:31.820
really acknowledging the
fact that we are providing

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:33.530
the networking
capabilities when it comes

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:37.010
to performance on a scale
to Kubernetes deployments

00:30:37.010 --> 00:30:39.950
natively, the same
way that we are doing

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:43.730
for the rest of
the VM deployments

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:46.710
or any other type of
deployments on our platform.

00:30:46.710 --> 00:30:52.480
So we really are going to
a model in which Kubernetes

00:30:52.480 --> 00:30:56.090
and the container deployments
are first-class citizens

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:58.220
for everything on
networking, which

00:30:58.220 --> 00:31:02.765
will allow our customers
to get advantage they want

00:31:02.765 --> 00:31:06.640
from the performance, the global
scale, the global reachability

00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:09.690
that Google network provides.

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:13.910
With that, I will wrap up here.

00:31:13.910 --> 00:31:16.850
Stay tuned for
live Q&amp;A because we

00:31:16.850 --> 00:31:19.700
are going to be back
in less than a minute.

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:20.300
Thank you.

00:32:43.915 --> 00:32:45.540
WESTON HUTCHINS: All
right, we're back.

00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:49.050
And we'll take some questions
that we had during the session.

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:51.000
So the first
question is, what are

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:53.880
the differences between
multi-zone and regional

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:55.110
clusters?

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:58.380
So before we launched
regional clusters,

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:01.200
we had this concept called
multi-zone clusters.

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:06.120
And what it allowed you to
do is take a zonal control

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:09.630
plan that ran in a single zone
and then create node pools

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:10.900
in different zones.

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:13.740
So if you had a cluster that you
wanted to create in US Central

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:16.680
1A, you could then
add a node pool

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:19.140
that was in US Central
IB or 1C or wherever you

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:21.510
wanted to go and create those.

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:24.540
With regional clusters, we
give you that functionality

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:26.050
automatically out of the box.

00:33:26.050 --> 00:33:28.140
So when you go and
create a cluster,

00:33:28.140 --> 00:33:30.090
it automatically
spreads your nodes

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:32.260
across the different
zones for you.

00:33:32.260 --> 00:33:34.991
The other major difference is
that we replicate the control

00:33:34.991 --> 00:33:35.490
plane.

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:39.150
So we spread the masters
across three different zones

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:42.730
in a region and we replicate
etcd between those.

00:33:42.730 --> 00:33:44.700
So multi-zone was
kind of the old way

00:33:44.700 --> 00:33:46.140
of doing it for zonal clusters.

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:48.300
And it's still really good for
test clusters or development

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:50.800
clusters where you just want
to spin it up and add some more

00:33:50.800 --> 00:33:52.620
capacity to try out
service discovery

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:54.150
across different zones.

00:33:54.150 --> 00:33:55.650
Regional clusters
is really what you

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:58.770
want to be using for the
increased availability and zero

00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:00.210
downtime that you
get for running

00:34:00.210 --> 00:34:02.864
production-grade applications.

00:34:02.864 --> 00:34:04.530
All right, so the
next question is, do I

00:34:04.530 --> 00:34:07.530
have to spread my nodes across
three zones in a region?

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:09.659
And I think this is probably
specifically talking

00:34:09.659 --> 00:34:12.090
about regional clusters.

00:34:12.090 --> 00:34:14.449
I mentioned that we
automatically create node pools

00:34:14.449 --> 00:34:17.260
in three zones in a
particular region,

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:20.320
but I do want to point out
that that's not a requirement.

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:23.909
So you can control both
where the nodes are created

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:27.340
and also how many nodes in each
of those zones are created.

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:29.760
So for example, by
passing in the flag,

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:31.830
there's a node
location flag that you

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:34.770
can use to actually create
a regional cluster that

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:37.158
is actually in a single zone.

00:34:37.158 --> 00:34:38.699
So you might want
to do this in order

00:34:38.699 --> 00:34:41.940
to save egress cross
between different zones

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:44.610
if you're not really sure about
how your cluster services are

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:47.190
communicating with one
another, or you just

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:48.870
want to keep everything
local to a zone,

00:34:48.870 --> 00:34:51.630
but you want to have the
additional availability

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:53.370
of a regional control plane.

00:34:53.370 --> 00:34:55.770
So it's really easy
to mix and match those

00:34:55.770 --> 00:34:58.999
two just by specifying
the node locations flag.

00:34:58.999 --> 00:35:00.540
And then if you want
to, you can even

00:35:00.540 --> 00:35:03.570
specify the number
of nodes flag, which

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:05.550
allows you to say
whether or not you

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:09.030
want 1, 2, 3, n number
of nodes created

00:35:09.030 --> 00:35:11.880
in each particular zone.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:14.760
INES ENVID: All right, so
I'll take the next question.

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:19.470
When should I use shared
VPC for my GKE deployments?

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:23.070
So typically, you
want to use shared VPC

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:28.020
as long as you have
administrative separations

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:31.110
that you want to
provide autonomy.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:34.980
And that could be
when you have, let's

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:37.380
say, a department A,
department B, that

00:35:37.380 --> 00:35:39.540
are running different
applications, or even

00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:45.360
to the granularity of a
specific application developer.

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:48.420
So you want to have application
A and application B separating

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:49.590
their own projects.

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:55.290
You can actually partition your
deployments to the granularity

00:35:55.290 --> 00:35:56.310
that it suits you.

00:35:56.310 --> 00:36:00.990
The shared VPC is going to
allow you to communicate really

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:03.930
those applications
that you are separating

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:05.250
for administrative reasons.

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:10.230
So we see different use cases,
from having two or three

00:36:10.230 --> 00:36:12.460
big departments, or
maybe an environment

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:15.300
that is test, stage,
and production that

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:18.000
are separated and communicated
through a shared VPC.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:21.290
Or we see to the granularity
of different developers

00:36:21.290 --> 00:36:25.020
that are managing different
applications being separated

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:27.420
and connected
through a shared VPC.

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:36.300
So every time that you want to
provide network communication

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:41.300
that is private, that is
provided to your developers

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:43.812
as a service that they
have available to them--

00:36:43.812 --> 00:36:45.270
that's where they
need to connect--

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:48.360
and you want to
keep them isolated,

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:52.140
in the sense of administratively
for having their own billing

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:55.030
quota and IM so
that they are owners

00:36:55.030 --> 00:36:57.450
of their own resources,
that's where your shared VPC

00:36:57.450 --> 00:37:01.310
models come in place.

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:02.850
I'll take the next
question as well.

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:04.720
If we expose
services inside VPC,

00:37:04.720 --> 00:37:08.450
can they just stay
internal to the enterprise?

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:09.260
Absolutely.

00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:14.070
So this is actually one of
the most prevalent models

00:37:14.070 --> 00:37:17.210
in enterprise deployment.

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:23.030
There is the capability
to have your--

00:37:23.030 --> 00:37:26.930
through the configuration of
the [? IS ?] IP that we just

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:31.340
talked about, you configure
your infrastructure VMs.

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:33.740
You configure your
pods and your services

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:38.270
as private IPs, configured as
part of the same VPC subnet.

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:41.540
And then you decide
which services

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:42.770
are going to be exposed.

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:45.500
Now, the fact that you're
exposing those services,

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:50.450
it totally doesn't need to
mean that you are exposing them

00:37:50.450 --> 00:37:51.290
publicly.

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:53.795
You can expose them
within your VPC.

00:37:53.795 --> 00:37:56.090
And because you now
have a shared VPC,

00:37:56.090 --> 00:37:58.610
really you have a model in
which those services communicate

00:37:58.610 --> 00:38:03.210
privately to your enterprise
across your different cluster

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:04.860
deployments.

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:09.320
And the last one, why are
native VPC clusters more secure?

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:14.660
One of the big things with
enabling with native VPC

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:22.560
clusters is the fact that
we, as a networking stack,

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:29.420
we understand where the pods
and where each of their services

00:38:29.420 --> 00:38:34.370
are really located as part
of our infrastructure.

00:38:34.370 --> 00:38:38.090
So what it means is that
we are able to understand

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:42.920
that applications are
hosted on a specific node,

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:46.160
on a specific host of
our infrastructure.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:51.440
In doing so, we are then able
to apply security checks,

00:38:51.440 --> 00:38:53.120
like IP spoofing.

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:56.180
And when we're actually allowing
traffic to a specific node,

00:38:56.180 --> 00:38:59.000
we are allowing only traffic
for applications that having

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:01.320
configure as part of that node.

00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:06.087
So if I have, let's say,
pod one with IP 10.0.0.1,

00:39:06.087 --> 00:39:08.670
then I'm going to make a check
that the traffic [? destined ?]

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:13.640
to that node is
[? destined ?] to a pod IP

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:14.730
that it's hosting there.

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:17.790
And if not, I'm not going to
allow that traffic to come in.

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:21.782
So we really natively ensure
that the traffic is legit

00:39:21.782 --> 00:39:23.990
when they're communicating
through our infrastructure

00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:27.259
because we understand where
applications are hosting.

00:39:27.259 --> 00:39:29.300
WESTON HUTCHINS: If you
have any other questions,

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:31.420
we'll be online afterwards
to help answer them.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.461
You can use the platform,
type in your questions,

00:39:33.461 --> 00:39:36.090
and we'll jump on with any
other thing that you have.

00:39:36.090 --> 00:39:39.320
Now, stay tuned for the
next session, coming live

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:42.290
from Sunnyvale, "Why
APIs are products, not

00:39:42.290 --> 00:39:43.380
one-off projects."

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:45.110
Thank you very much.

